---
/**
 * The masthead, on every tool page.
 *
 * Third shape, and the first one that is not trying to be a navigation bar.
 *
 * It began as a copy of seattlemakers.org's header - a near-black full-bleed
 * slab with the lockup knocked out in white - then went light so it matched
 * the pages under it. What was left over was the listing of every tool across
 * the top, which is a lot of chrome for a site with two of them, and a stacked
 * two-line lockup big enough to be the loudest thing in the bar.
 *
 * So it is an identity strip now: the monogram, the name of the thing, and the
 * one link that leaves. Navigation lives on the page - the index *is* the list
 * of tools, and each tool page carries a single `.sm-back` to it. Chrome that
 * duplicates the page's own content earns nothing.
 *
 * The mark is the "m" of "makers" cropped straight out of wordmark.png rather
 * than drawn fresh, so it is the real letterform, outline and all. Using the
 * full lockup here would have set "Seattle Makers" twice - once as artwork and
 * again as the title beside it.
 *
 * Styles live in global.css, not in a scoped block here, so the bar can be
 * reasoned about alongside the sheet it sits on.
 */
---

<header class="sm-masthead no-print">
  <div class="sm-masthead-inner">
    <a class="sm-brand" href="/">
      {/* Decorative: the title beside it already names the site, and a screen
          reader announcing "Seattle Makers" twice helps nobody. */}
      <img class="sm-mark" src="/brand/mark.png" alt="" />
      <span class="sm-brand-name">Seattle Makers <span>Web Tools</span></span>
    </a>
    <a class="sm-nav-out" href="https://seattlemakers.org" rel="noopener">
      seattlemakers.org
      <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span>
    </a>
  </div>
</header>
